<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bes was an ancient Egyptian protective deity, shown as a bearded dwarf with a lion-like face, who guarded households, women in childbirth and children against evil. His cult spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> through Phoenician trade. The Punic city of Ebusus on Ibiza put his image on its coins, and the island's name is often explained as 'island of Bes'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bes.jpg" alt="Bes" width="320" height="661" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eternal Space · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deity_Bes.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Bes, also written Bisu, was an ancient Egyptian deity, paired with a female counterpart, Beset, and probably of Nubian or C-Group origin. People in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> venerated him long before those in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>, which points to a southern origin. In Nubian the word bes means cat, and the god is regularly shown with feline features. The feathered crown he wears was also common dress in ancient Kush. Donald Mackenzie suggested in 1907 that Bes was a Middle Kingdom import from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, whose cult spread widely only at the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, but figurines resembling Bes, thirteen of them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-el-farkha">Tell el-Farkha</a>, have since been found in deposits of the pre-dynastic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqada">Naqada</a> period.</p><p>Bes is first mentioned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyramid-texts">Pyramid Texts</a> but seems to have been best known and most widely worshipped in the Middle Kingdom. Evidence of his worship continues into Coptic Egypt, the latest being a jug showing Bes from Tell Edfu, dated to the 10th century CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Bes protected households and, in particular, mothers, children and childbirth. His tasks included killing snakes, driving off evil spirits and watching over children, and he was present with Taweret at births. Where Egyptian gods were normally shown in profile, Bes appeared in a full-face portrait, ithyphallic and sometimes in a soldier's tunic, ready to attack any approaching evil. Because he drove off evil, he also came to symbolise the good things in life, music, dance and sexual pleasure. Some modern scholars, among them James Romano, hold that in its earliest form Bes represented a lion rearing on its hind legs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Images of Bes were kept in homes. From the Middle Kingdom he appears on masks, amulets, infant feeding bottles, magic knives, furniture, toiletries and game pieces, and many jugs and storage jars bear his image. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> dancers, musicians and servant girls wore tattoos of him on the thigh, as a wall painting of the 19th Dynasty from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-medina">Deir el-Medina</a> shows. In the Ptolemaic period chambers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a> were painted with Bes and Beset, which Egyptologists think were used for curing fertility problems or for healing rituals.</p><p>His worship spread abroad. Beset was more popular in Minoan Crete, while Bes found favour with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> and the ancient Cypriots. From the end of the 6th century BCE his image spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, to which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> then belonged, and has been found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> and as far off as central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. Over time he came to be shown in Persian clothes and headdress. His cult reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balearic-islands">Balearic Islands</a> as well.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
